  "generated_description": "<div class=\"mountain-description prose prose-stone max-w-none\">\n  <p><strong>Coyote Bluff</strong> is a 3,570-foot summit in Texas. The Coyote Bluff Trail covers 5.0 miles round trip (out-and-back) with about 52 feet of elevation gain \u2014 a gradual walk with modest climbing.</p>\n  <h3>Getting There</h3><p>The trailhead is at <a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=35.29961,-101.97061\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">35.29961, -101.97061</a> (tap for driving directions). It's roughly 225 miles east of Santa Fe, New Mexico. Arrive early on weekends \u2014 trailhead lots at popular peaks fill quickly, and cell service is often unreliable once you leave the highway.</p>\n  <h3>When to Go</h3><p>October through April is the comfortable window here \u2014 summer temperatures at this elevation are dangerously hot, so if you must hike in summer, start at first light and carry more water than you think you need.</p>\n  <h3>Know Before You Go</h3><p>The Easy rating comes from the National Park Service difficulty formula, which weighs total distance against elevation gain. Most hikers should budget about 1.5 hours of moving time; add margin for breaks, photos and the descent. Download the GPX track from this page before you go \u2014 cell coverage is unreliable in the mountains and a saved route works offline in any GPS app. Conditions change: verify current access, closures and weather with the official land manager before you go. Trail data is sourced from OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL).</p>\n  <p>Looking to link objectives or plan a weekend? Nearby summits include Wolf Mountain, John Ray and Lost Mesa \u2014 each has its own guide on this site.</p>\n</div>",
